window.PHASE_DATA_LN=window.PHASE_DATA_LN||{};window.PHASE_DATA_LN[5]={"pages":[{"pdf":117,"printed":102,"ch":5,"text":"CHAPTER 5\n\nLayer 4: Financial Contagion and Systemic Risk\nBefore you start\nLayer 4 is about how one bank's trouble reaches others. You can read it on its own, but it helps to know the five ideas below and to have done Layer 3 (capital) first. The papers are theoretical and mathematical; these notes give the logic and keep the algebra light.\n\nConcepts you need\nTable 5.1. Before you start: Concepts you need\nConcept\n\nPlain meaning\n\nWhere it appears\n\nInterbank\nexposure\n\nOne bank lends to, or holds a deposit in, another;\na loss at the borrower is a loss for the lender\n\nAll four papers\n\nLiquidity vs\nsolvency\n\nIlliquid: cannot pay now. Insolvent: assets worth\nless than debts. A bank can be one without the\nother\n\nAllen–Gale (liquidity);\nEisenberg–Noe, Acemoglu et\nal. (solvency)\n\nSeniority and\nSenior creditors (for example depositors) are paid\npro-rata payment first; junior creditors share what is left in\nproportion to their claims\n\nEisenberg–Noe, Acemoglu et\nal.\n\nFixed point /\nclearing\n\nEveryone's payment depends on everyone else's, so Eisenberg–Noe\nyou solve for the set of payments that is consistent\nwith all of them\n\nMargin, haircut,\nfire sale\n\nMargin or haircut = the part of an asset's price\nyou cannot borrow against; a fire sale is a forced\nsale at a low price\n\nNetwork shapes\n\nRing (each bank owes one other), complete (each Allen–Gale, Acemoglu et al.\nowes all others equally), disconnected or\nclustered\n\nBrunnermeier–Pedersen\n\nA basic grasp of a deposit contract (promise to pay c1 on demand) helps for Allen–Gale. If you did Layer 3, you already know capital as a buffer and Basel's risk weights, which Brunnermeier– Pedersen use in Appendix A.2.\n\nMap of the four papers"},{"pdf":118,"printed":103,"ch":5,"text":"Table 5.2. Before you start: Map of the four papers\nPaper\n\nQuestion\n\nType of link\n\nMath level\n\nAllen–Gale (2000) Do banks that insure each other\nagainst local liquidity shocks make\nthe system fragile?\n\nInterbank\ndeposits\n\nModerate; mostly logic\nand diagrams\n\nEisenberg–Noe\n(2001)\n\nGiven a web of debts, what does\neach bank actually pay?\n\nInterbank debts\n\nHigher; fixed-point and\nmatrix algebra\n\nAcemoglu et al.\n(2015)\n\nWhich network shapes handle small\nand large shocks best?\n\nUnsecured\ninterbank loans\n\nHigher; proofs in\nappendix\n\nBrunnermeier–\nPedersen (2009)\n\nHow do funding limits and market\nliquidity feed each other?\n\nPrices, margins,\nModerate; intuition-led\nhaircuts (no direct with numerical\nlinks)\nexamples\n\nSuggested reading order\n1. Allen–Gale first: the cleanest story of contagion, and the others refer to it.\n2. Eisenberg–Noe: the payment-clearing engine behind network stress tests.\n3. Acemoglu et al.: builds on both; it explains when density helps and when it hurts.\n4. Brunnermeier–Pedersen: a different channel (prices and funding). It can be read any time after Allen–Gale. If time is short, read sections 1 and 3 of these notes first, then the master quotables in section 5.\n\nFile and page notes\n• Eisenberg–Noe's PDF has a damaged text layer; I used OCR, so check quotes against the page.\n• Brunnermeier–Pedersen is the Advance Access version (pages 1–38), so confirm journal page numbers before citing.\n• Allen–Gale and Acemoglu et al. are the printed journal pages.\n\n1. Allen and Gale (2000), Financial Contagion\nJournal of Political Economy 108(1), 1–33. Page numbers below are the printed journal pages (1–33). The one-line message. Banks insure each other against local liquidity shocks by holding each other's deposits. That works perfectly in normal times, but if the economy as a whole runs short of liquidity, the same cross-holdings carry a small shock from one region to every other region. Whether this happens depends on the pattern of the claims, not only on their size.\n\n1.1 The question and the answer (pp. 1–6)\n• Earlier bank-run theory (Diamond–Dybvig) treats one bank. Allen–Gale ask how a crisis passes between banks when the only link is financial claims."},{"pdf":119,"printed":104,"ch":5,"text":"• They use a stylised model with no panics, no sunspots and no information problems. Contagion is shown as the only possible outcome (every continuation equilibrium), not one of several.\n• Their finding in one sentence: contagion needs (i) an aggregate liquidity shortage, and (ii) an interbank market that is incomplete but connected. ”A small liquidity preference shock in one region can spread by contagion throughout the economy. The possibility of contagion depends strongly on the completeness of the structure of interregional claims. Complete claims structures are shown to be more robust than incomplete structures.” (p. 1) [QUOTE: the thesis-ready summary]\n\nTwo points from the introduction worth keeping (pp. 4–5): cross-holdings of deposits ”cannot increase the total amount of liquidity” in the system, and when banks liquidate claims on each other at the same moment this ”merely denies liquidity” to the troubled region. The paper also gives a free-rider reason: each bank prefers to protect its own long asset and let others bear the liquidation.\n\n1.2 The model (pp. 6–10)\nTable 5.3. Allen and Gale (2000): 1.2 The model (pp. 6–10)\nElement\n\nSetup\n\nDates\n\n0 (invest), 1 (early withdrawals), 2 (late withdrawals)\n\nAssets\n\nShort asset: 1 unit today gives 1 unit tomorrow. Long asset: 1 unit today gives R > 1 at date 2, but only r < 1 if liquidated at date 1\n\nRegions\n\nFour, A, B, C, D, each with many identical banks and consumers\n\nShocks\n\nA consumer is ”early” (needs money at date 1) or ”late”. The early fraction is ωH in a high region and ωL in a low one. A and C are high when B and D are low, and vice versa (Table 1). The average across regions is a constant γ\n\nContract\n\nBanks offer a demand deposit paying c1 at date 1 or c2 at date 2\n\nKey logic. Because the regional shocks are perfectly negatively correlated, the economy has no aggregate uncertainty, even though each region does. A planner can therefore insure every region fully (Proposition 1, p. 9: the first-best is also incentive-efficient, so late consumers do not run).\n\n1.3 How banks decentralise risk sharing (pp. 10–15)\nBanks do it by holding deposits in other regions' banks (interbank deposits). A region short of liquidity withdraws from a region that has a surplus. Two structures achieve the same first-best:\n1. Complete market (Fig. 1). Each bank holds z = (ωH − γ)/2 in each of the other regions.\n2. Incomplete market (Fig. 2). Each bank holds z = ωH − γ in one neighbour only, in a chain A→B→C→D→A.\n3. Disconnected incomplete (Fig. 3). A and B hold each other's deposits, and C and D hold each other's. This also gets the first-best (p. 14–15)."},{"pdf":120,"printed":105,"ch":5,"text":"In normal states (S1, S2) all three look the same. Their fragility differs sharply. The authors stress this: the structures ”turn out to have striking differences for financial fragility” (p. 15).\n\n1.4 The fragility experiment (pp. 15–22)\nThe authors add a state S with probability zero in which total demand for liquidity is slightly higher: region A has γ + ε early consumers, the other three have γ (Table 2, p. 16). Total liquidity is now short. Pecking order (p. 17). Bank-by-bank, the cheapest source of cash at date 1 is the short asset (cost 1), then liquidating interbank deposits (cost c2/c1), and last the long asset (cost R/r). So 1 < c2/c1 < R/r. Banks always sell short assets first, then deposits, and the long asset only last. Bank states (p. 17). Solvent: pays everyone from short assets and deposits. Insolvent: must sell some long asset. Bankrupt: cannot pay even by selling everything. Buffer (p. 19). The buffer b(ω) = r[x − (1 − ω)c1/R] is how much cash a bank can raise from the long asset without making late consumers run (they must still be promised c1 at date 2). The contagion chain (pp. 19–22).\n1. If ε·c1 is within region A's buffer (condition 8), A is insolvent but nobody else is hurt.\n2. If ε·c1 exceeds the buffer (condition 9), A is bankrupt. Every bank wants to pull its deposits from every other bank at once, so mutual withdrawals net to zero and no region can import liquidity.\n3. Because A is bankrupt, a deposit in A is worth q_A < c1. Region D holds z of them, so it loses at least z(c1 − q_A).\n4. If that loss exceeds D's buffer (condition 11), D is bankrupt too, then C, then B. The spillover grows with each failure. Proposition 2 (p. 21). With the chain structure of Fig. 2, a zero-probability state S, and conditions (9) and (11), ”the banks in all regions must go bankrupt at date 1 in state S.” [QUOTE: formal contagion result]\n\nLow r makes this worse twice: the loss on A's liquidated assets is bigger and every buffer is smaller (p. 20).\n\n1.5 Why completeness helps, and why disconnection helps too (pp. 22–24)\n• Robustness (Sec. VI, pp. 22–23). With the complete structure each bank holds z/2 in each of three regions. The loss on any one failed region is (z/2)(c1 − q_A*), which can fit inside the buffer (condition 13) even when conditions (8) and (11) fail. The exposure to any single bank is smaller, so the shock is shared out.\n• Containment (Sec. VII, pp. 23–24). In the disconnected structure (Fig. 3) a shock in A may hit B but ”there is no reason why [it] should spread any further” (p. 23).\n• Non-monotone result (p. 24). Crisis is confined to A with complete markets, hits all four regions with the chain, and hits only A and B when disconnected."},{"pdf":121,"printed":106,"ch":5,"text":"”Contagion depends on the endogenous pattern of financial claims.” (p. 24) [QUOTE: structure, not size, drives contagion]\n\nNote the caveat (pp. 24): complete markets also have equilibria with contagion, because banks can choose overlapping claims. The chain of Fig. 2 matters because it is the only pattern that market structure allows.\n\n1.6 Could other interbank instruments avoid it? (pp. 24–27)\n• Contingent or non-demandable claims (pp. 25–26). No. If banks may refuse to\npay, they will do so whenever the alternative is selling the long asset, which has the same effect as netting claims. The paper says the use of demand deposits in the interbank market ”is not analogous to” their use in the retail market (p. 25). Suspension of convertibility prevents runs in retail but not in the interbank case.\n• Ex post lending (pp. 26–27). An interbank loan market at date 1 stops spillover but cannot cure the region in trouble. With an economy-wide cash shortage the loan rate would be R/r, which is too high to help A. Ex post markets also face lemons and hold-up problems, and cannot share asset-return risk.\n\n1.7 Discussion and extensions (pp. 27–32)\n• Equilibrium and the planner (pp. 27–29). Making the shock symmetric (any region can be hit with probability δ, Table 3) does not change things for small δ. Even a planner choosing date-0 portfolios, but working through demand deposits, allows contagion because insuring against rare shocks means holding more of the low-return liquid asset, which is not worth it. So contagion can be ”constrained efficient” (p. 29).\n• Many regions (pp. 29–30). With complete markets, adding regions makes a single shock negligible: the system is robust. With incomplete markets, a single small shock can spread to an arbitrarily large number of regions.\n• Sunspots and risky assets (p. 31). A multiple-equilibria reading is possible but not needed. Holding claims on banks is much more dangerous than holding the assets directly: a bank lending directly into another region's long asset can wait until date 2 and earn R, while interbank claims get marked down to the liquidation value.\n• Central bank (p. 32). ”One way of thinking about the role of a central bank is to complete markets”: every bank has a link to it, so the central bank can stop inefficient liquidation.\n\n1.8 Limits to remember when you cite this paper\n• It is a liquidity model, not a solvency model: banks fail because depositors want cash, not because assets lose value (the extension to risky assets is only sketched, p. 31).\n• The contagion state S has probability zero, and results for small positive δ are argued, not fully proved (p. 28).\n• Regions are symmetric and only four; the 'many regions' result is an extension (pp. 29– 30).\n• No capital, no leverage, no regulation. This is why Layers 3 (capital) and 4 (networks) need to be read together."},{"pdf":122,"printed":107,"ch":5,"text":"1.9 Quotables from this paper\nTable 5.4. Allen and Gale (2000): 1.9 Quotables from this paper\n\nQuote or point\n\nPage\n\nUse in thesis\n\n”A small liquidity preference shock in one region can\nspread by contagion throughout the economy…”\n\nCore definition of\ncontagion\n\nCross-holdings cannot increase total liquidity; mutual\nliquidation merely denies liquidity to the troubled\nregion\n\n4–5\n\nWhy interbank links\ncan amplify\n\nPecking order: short asset, then deposits, then long\nasset (1 < c2/c1 < R/r)\n\nFire-sale logic\n\nProposition 2: all regions go bankrupt in state S\n\nFormal result\n\nContagion depends on the endogenous pattern of\nfinancial claims\n\nNetwork structure\nmatters\n\nDemand deposits in interbank markets are not\nanalogous to retail deposits\n\nWhy suspension doesn't\nwork\n\nComplete markets: more regions make a single shock\nnegligible; incomplete markets can propagate to\narbitrarily many regions\n\nLink to Acemoglu et al.\n(2015)\n\nA central bank can be thought of as completing\nmarkets\n\nPolicy link (lender of\nlast resort, RBI)\n\nBridge to the next paper. Allen–Gale show contagion with four symmetric regions and a single type of claim. Eisenberg–Noe (next) give a general method for any network of debts: how much each bank can actually pay when everyone owes everyone.\n\n2. Eisenberg and Noe (2001), Systemic Risk in Financial Systems\nManagement Science 47(2), 236–249. Page numbers are the printed pages. File note: the PDF in your folder has a broken text layer, so I read it by OCR (page images). Wording of quotes was taken from the OCR text; check against the page before quoting verbatim. The one-line message. When firms owe each other money in loops (A owes B, B owes C, C owes A), you cannot read off who pays what. Eisenberg–Noe prove that a consistent set of payments, the clearing payment vector, always exists, is usually unique, and can be found by a simple round-by-round algorithm that also ranks firms by how exposed they are to others' defaults. This is the mathematical engine behind Acemoglu et al. (2015). Their ”payment equilibrium” generalises it (Acemoglu et al., p. 571).\n\n2.1 Problem and contribution (pp. 236–238)\n• Firms' values depend on what other firms pay them, which depends on still other firms. The authors call this cyclical interdependence: ”A default by Firm A on its obligations to Firm B may lead B to default on its obligations to C. A default by C may, in turn, have a feedback effect on A.” (p. 236)"},{"pdf":123,"printed":108,"ch":5,"text":"• Real settings: bank payment systems (CHIPS, Fedwire), clearing houses. Historical failures cited include Herstatt (1974), the Bank of New York overnight shortfall of $22.6 billion (1985), the US S&L bailout (about $500 billion), LTCM, and the 1982 al-Manakh market collapse in Kuwait, where gross mutual liabilities exceeded four times Kuwait's GDP (pp. 236–237).\n• The authors say that, to their knowledge, theirs is ”the first to analyze, in a general fashion, the properties of intercorporate cash flows in financial systems featuring cyclical interdependence” (p. 237).\n\n2.2 The model (pp. 238–240)\nTable 5.5. Eisenberg and Noe (2001): 2.2 The model (pp. 238–240)\nObject\n\nMeaning\n\nn nodes\n\nFirms or banks in one clearing system\n\nL (n × n)\n\nNominal liabilities: L_ij = what node i owes node j. Non-negative, zero diagonal\n\nOperating cash flow from outside the system, e ≥ 0 for each node\n\np̄_i\n\nTotal obligation of node i: sum of L_ij over j\n\nRelative liabilities matrix: Π_ij = L_ij / p̄_i, so rows sum to 1\n\nVector of actual total payments by each node\n\nInflow to node i = (sum over j of Π_ji p_j) + e_i. Equity of node i = inflow minus payment, floored at zero (p. 239). Three rules of a clearing vector (Definition 1, p. 239).\n1. Limited liability. A node never pays more than it has.\n2. Absolute priority. Debt is paid before equity: either the node pays its obligation in full, or equity is zero (all value goes to creditors).\n3. Proportionality. If a node defaults, all creditor nodes are paid pro rata to their nominal claim (equal priority). Fixed-point form (p. 240). Each node pays ”the minimum of what it has and what it owes”: p = Φ(p) = (Πᵀp + e) ∧ p̄. A clearing vector is a fixed point of Φ.\n\n2.3 Main results (pp. 240–244)\n1. Existence (Theorem 1, p. 240). Φ is increasing, concave and nonexpansive, so by Tarski's fixed-point theorem a greatest and a least clearing vector always exist. Equity at each node is the same under all clearing vectors.\n2. Uniqueness (pp. 241–242). Not guaranteed in general (an example is in Appendix 2, p. 249). It holds under regularity: every node's risk orbit (all nodes reachable by some chain of debts) contains a node with positive operating cash flow, so that value from somewhere can reach every part of the system. Theorem 2 (p. 242): in a regular system the greatest and least clearing vectors coincide.\n3. Fictitious default algorithm (pp. 243–244)."},{"pdf":124,"printed":109,"ch":5,"text":"• Round 1: assume everyone pays in full; find who cannot (first-order defaults).\n• Round 2: recompute payments assuming only those nodes default; find new defaults (second-order).\n• Repeat. Lemma 3 (p. 243): this converges to the clearing vector in at most n steps.\n4. Systemic-risk ranking (p. 244). The round in which a node defaults is a measure of its exposure: first-round defaults are ”fundamentally insolvent”; later waves are nodes that fail only because others failed.\n5. Programming characterisation (p. 244). Maximising any strictly increasing function of payments subject to limited liability gives the clearing vector (Lemma 4). The authors note that Kuwait's authorities used this approach to clear the al-Manakh debts (p. 245).\n\n2.4 Comparative statics (pp. 245–247)\n• Lemma 5 (p. 245). The clearing vector is a concave, increasing, nonexpansive function of operating cash flows and of nominal liabilities. Concave means that adding a unit of cash helps less than losing a unit hurts.\n• Consequence 1: more volatile cash flows (in the second-order-dominance sense) reduce expected debt payments (p. 245–246).\n• Consequence 2: total system value falls with volatility even with no bankruptcy costs. The Corollary (p. 246): ”Increases in the volatility… of operating cash flows lowers the total value of all nodes in the system.”\n• Equity is ambiguous (p. 246). Higher risk lowers payments to a node (bad for net creditors) and payments by it (good for net debtors). So some firms' equity can fall even though the total is unchanged.\n• Lemma 6 (p. 247). A mixture of two systems cannot do better than the better one or worse than the worse one on payments; a stronger concavity result fails. The authors conclude that ”using changes in total asset values to measure the effect of an economic shock on a group of tightly interconnected companies (e.g., Japanese banks) can be highly misleading.” (p. 238) [QUOTE: why firm-by-firm event studies can mislead]\n\n2.5 Extensions the authors suggest (pp. 247–248)\n• Valuing debt and equity and computing default probabilities, default correlation and value-at-risk with stochastic cash flows (p. 247).\n• Cross-holdings of equity; several priority classes; violations of absolute priority.\n• Dynamics: nodes that default become owned by creditors; a ”central bank” lends at a market rate. They call this the hardest extension (p. 248).\n\n2.6 Limits when you cite it\n• One clearing date, no liquidation, no fire sales. There are no bankruptcy costs; value is conserved. Contagion here is purely redistribution of fixed value. Acemoglu et al. add costly liquidation and outside senior claims."},{"pdf":125,"printed":110,"ch":5,"text":"• Network and cash flows are given (p. 239). The paper explains why firms would still pay out ex post: ex ante contracts are assumed perfectly enforceable (p. 240).\n• Uniqueness needs regularity. Without it, many payment vectors can be consistent (App. 2, p. 249).\n• It is a computation and existence paper; it makes no claim about which network shapes are safer. That is the contribution of Allen–Gale and Acemoglu et al.\n\n2.7 Worked example in words (p. 249, Appendix 2)\nTwo banks, each owing the other 1.00, with no outside cash. Any p = t(1,1) for t in [0,1] clears, so payments are indeterminate. Give node 1 one cent of outside cash and the only clearing vector is (1.00, 1.00) because anything less would leave node 1 with equity while not fully paying its debt, which absolute priority forbids.\n\n2.8 Quotables from this paper\nTable 5.6. Eisenberg and Noe (2001): 2.8 Quotables from this paper\n\nQuote or point\n\nPage\n\nUse in thesis\n\nCyclical interdependence: A defaults on B, B on C, 236\nand C feeds back to A\n\nDefines the feedback loop in\nbank networks\n\nFirst general analysis of intercorporate cash flows\nwith cyclical interdependence (the authors' claim)\n\n237\n\nPosition in the literature\n\nClearing vector: three rules (limited liability,\nabsolute priority, proportionality)\n\n239\n\nMethod for any network\nsimulation\n\nEach node pays ”the minimum of what it has and\nwhat it owes”\n\n240\n\nIntuitive statement of the\nfixed point\n\nClearing vector exists; unique under regularity\n\n240–242\n\nJustify network simulations\nare well-defined\n\nFictitious default algorithm converges in at most n\nrounds; default round = exposure to systemic risk\n\n243–244\n\nStress-test method (waves\nof default)\n\nVolatility lowers total system value even without\nbankruptcy costs\n\n246\n\nRisk without dissipation\n\nTotal asset value changes can be misleading for\ninterconnected firms\n\n237–238\n\nCaution on event studies of\nbank returns\n\nBridge to the next paper. Eisenberg–Noe take the network as given and find who pays what. Acemoglu et al. (previous section) ask which network shapes make cascades worse. The last paper, Brunnermeier–Pedersen, switches off direct links altogether: banks can be hit through market prices and funding.\n\n3. Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015), Systemic Risk and\nStability in Financial Networks\nAmerican Economic Review 105(2), 564–608. Page numbers are the printed AER pages. Main text is pp. 564–588; the rest is appendix and proofs (read for the statements, not the proofs)."},{"pdf":126,"printed":111,"ch":5,"text":"The one-line message. Dense interbank networks are shock absorbers when shocks are small and shock transmitters when shocks are large. The same structure that makes a system robust can make it fragile. The authors call this a phase transition. ”as long as the magnitude of negative shocks affecting financial institutions are sufficiently small, a more densely connected financial network… enhances financial stability. However, beyond a certain point, dense interconnections serve as a mechanism for the propagation of shocks, leading to a more fragile financial system.” (p. 564) [QUOTE: the abstract, thesis-ready]\n\n3.1 Why the paper exists (pp. 564–568)\n• After 2008, ”the architecture of the financial system plays a central role in shaping systemic risk” had become conventional wisdom, but its exact role ”remains, at best, imperfectly understood” (p. 564).\n• Two camps: (a) Allen–Gale and Freixas–Parigi–Rochet: more interconnection spreads losses over more creditors, so it is stabilising. (b) Vivier-Lirimont, Blume et al., and others: more counterparties means a higher chance of systemic collapse, like an epidemic (p. 565).\n• This paper reconciles the two: both are right, depending on shock size (p. 566).\n• It also confirms Haldane's (2009) ”robust-yet-fragile” conjecture: connections act as shock-absorbers within a range, then ”the system [flips to] the wrong side of the knifeedge” (p. 566).\n• A policy footnote (p. 564, fn 2): Dodd-Frank's single-counterparty exposure limits were motivated by this view.\n\n3.2 The model (pp. 569–572)\nTable 5.7. Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015): 3.2 The model (pp. 569–572) Piece\n\nWhat it is\n\nBanks\n\nn risk-neutral banks, 3 periods (t = 0, 1, 2)\n\nProject\n\nEach bank invests; it pays random z_i at t = 1 and a fixed non-pledgeable A at t = 2. It can be liquidated early at t = 1 for only a fraction ζ < 1\n\nInterbank debt\n\nBank j owes y_ij to bank i (face value). All junior creditors (other banks) have equal seniority\n\nSenior claims\n\nEach bank also owes an outside obligation v > 0 (retail deposits, wages, taxes, secured creditors) that is paid first\n\nNetwork\n\nWeighted directed graph of the debts y_ij\n\nBecause long-run returns are not pledgeable, all debts must be settled at t = 1. A bank that cannot pay liquidates its project (eq. 1, p. 570), and if that is not enough it defaults. Junior creditors are then paid pro rata, but only if the senior claim v is covered (eq. 2, p. 571). Payment equilibrium (Definition 2, p. 571). A set of mutually consistent repayments and liquidations. It generalises the clearing vector of Eisenberg–Noe (2001) by adding seniority and costly liquidation (p. 571). Proposition 1 (p. 572): an equilibrium always exists and is generically unique."},{"pdf":127,"printed":112,"ch":5,"text":"Network types (p. 570).\n• Regular network: every bank has the same total claims and liabilities y, so differences in fragility come only from who owes whom, not from size or leverage.\n• Ring: each bank owes only one creditor. The sparsest regular network.\n• Complete: each bank's debt is split equally across the other n − 1 banks. The densest.\n• γ-convex combination of ring and complete: moves from ring to complete as γ rises (Definition 1, p. 570). Shock setup (p. 572). Short-term returns are a (normal) or a − ε (shocked). The critical size is ε* = n(a − v), the total excess liquidity in the system (p. 574).\n\n3.3 Results, step by step (pp. 573–585)\n1. More interbank debt, more fragility (Prop. 3, p. 574). Scaling all liabilities up by β > 1 makes the system less stable and less resilient, whatever its structure.\n2. **Small shocks (ε < ε; Prop. 4, p. 574).* The ring is the least stable and resilient network, the complete network the most. The γ-combination gets better as γ rises. Logic: spreading losses over many creditors lets excess liquidity absorb them; in a ring, losses go entirely to one creditor.\n3. Majorization (Prop. 5, p. 576). Any mixing of the distressed bank's liabilities into a more even pattern does not increase defaults, for small shocks. A caution (Example, p. 576): equalising a bank's two exposures can still raise fragility if it starves the bank that needed the resources.\n4. **Large shocks (ε > ε; Prop. 6, pp. 577–578).* The picture flips: the complete and ring networks are now the least stable. Any δ-connected (weakly linked clusters) network is strictly better than both. ”the complete network exhibits a form of phase transition: it flips from being the most to the least stable and resilient network… In particular, when ε > ε*, all banks in the complete network default.” (p. 578) [QUOTE: the phase transition in plain words]\n\n5. Two shock absorbers (p. 578). (i) Excess liquidity of non-distressed banks, used best in dense networks. (ii) Senior creditors' claims v: in a weakly connected network, outsiders (depositors, taxpayers) absorb some of the loss instead of other banks. When excess liquidity is exhausted, absorber (ii) matters, and it works best in sparse networks.\n6. Harmonic distance (Def. 6, Prop. 8, pp. 579–580). m_ij = 1 + Σ (y_ik / y)\nm_kj. It equals the mean hitting time of a random walk on the debt network (Prop. A1, p. 588). Banks closer to a distressed bank default first. The bank closest to all others is the ”too-interconnected-to-fail” bank.\n7. Why not eigenvector centrality (p. 580). Standard centrality measures suit linear interactions. With debt contracts the interactions are non-linear, so harmonic distance, not centrality, identifies systemic importance.\n8. Bottleneck parameter φ (Def. 7, Lemma 1, Cor. 2–3, pp. 580–582). φ is the\nsmallest amount of cross-linkage you must cut to split the network into two roughly equal halves. High φ (dense) means everyone defaults under a large shock; low φ (modular) means someone survives."},{"pdf":128,"printed":113,"ch":5,"text":"9. Multiple shocks (Prop. 9, pp. 582–583). The threshold becomes ε*_p = n(a −\nv)/p, falling with the number of shocks p. So the number of shocks acts like the size of shocks. A surprise: with several shocks and moderate y, the ring can beat the complete network (part iii).\n10. Positive liquidation value (Prop. 10–11, pp. 583–584). If ζ > 0 and partial liquidation is allowed, the transition gets smoother (an intermediate regime). If liquidation must be full, the sharp transition returns.\n11. Size heterogeneity (Prop. 12, p. 585). Bigger banks are more vulnerable to contagion, in a size-adjusted harmonic distance.\n\n3.4 Policy implications (pp. 586–587)\n”imposing limits on the exposure between pairs of financial institutions motivated by the possibility of small shocks may be counterproductive if the shocks are in fact large.” (p. 587) [QUOTE: regulation depends on shock size]\n\n• Ex ante: regulating the extent of interbank linkage must be informed by the expected size of shocks.\n• Ex post: injecting funds into, or bailing out, the systemically important (closest by harmonic distance) institutions contains contagion, though with moral hazard (p. 587).\n• Working-paper result (p. 587): with more than three banks, a network-formation externality appears: borrowers do not internalise the effect on their creditors' creditors, so observed networks can be inefficient.\n\n3.5 Limits and what the authors say is missing (pp. 587–588)\n• Only counterparty risk via unsecured debt. Fire sales on common assets and withdrawal of liquidity (repo roll-over, haircuts) are named as open problems (p. 587). Those are precisely Brunnermeier–Pedersen's topic.\n• No maturity mismatch, no repos, and no distinction between bank types (p. 587–588).\n• The network is given, not chosen. There is no empirical test; the authors call a systematic empirical investigation ”an important area for research” (p. 588).\n• Stylised: n banks, two shock values, regular networks. The paper's own footnote says it uses the word ”bank” for ease only (p. 565, fn 3).\n\n3.6 Reading it against Allen–Gale\nTable 5.8. Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015): 3.6 Reading it against Allen–Gale Allen–Gale (2000)\n\nAcemoglu et al. (2015)\n\nLink\n\nInterbank deposits (liquidity\ninsurance)\n\nUnsecured interbank loans\n\nMechanism\n\nLiquidity shortage, fire sale of long\nasset\n\nCounterparty default, seniority, costly\nliquidation\n\nStructures\n\n4 regions: complete, chain,\ndisconnected\n\nAny network: ring, complete, δ-connected,\ngeneral"},{"pdf":129,"printed":114,"ch":5,"text":"Allen–Gale (2000)\n\nAcemoglu et al. (2015)\n\nKey variable\n\nCompleteness and connectedness\n\nShock size (and number) relative to excess\nliquidity\n\nVerdict\n\nComplete is robust, chain fragile,\ndisconnected contains\n\nComplete is robust only for small shocks;\nmodular is best for large ones\n\nAcemoglu et al. explicitly say Allen–Gale ”also note that compared to a four-bank ring network, a pairwise-connected… network can be less prone to financial contagion” but did not study shock size or the phase transition (p. 567, fn 5).\n\n3.7 Quotables from this paper\nTable 5.9. Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015): 3.7 Quotables from this paper\n\nQuote or point\n\nPage\n\nUse in thesis\n\nAbstract: dense networks stabilise small shocks\nbut propagate large ones\n\n564\n\nCore framing for climate\nshocks of unknown size\n\nRole of architecture ”remains, at best, imperfectly 564\nunderstood”\n\nJustify network analysis of\nbanks\n\nRobust-yet-fragile (Haldane 2009)\n\n566, 579\n\nPolicy language\n\nComplete network flips from most to least stable\n\n578\n\nPhase transition\n\nTwo shock absorbers: excess liquidity and senior\ncreditors\n\n578\n\nWhy size matters\n\nHarmonic distance beats off-the-shelf centrality\nfor systemic importance\n\n580\n\nIf you use network\nmeasures\n\nNumber of shocks acts like shock size (ε*_p =\nn(a − v)/p)\n\n582–583\n\nMany banks hit by climate\nevents at once\n\nExposure limits for small shocks can be\ncounterproductive for large ones\n\n587\n\nRegulation design\n\nFire sales and liquidity withdrawal left for future\nresearch\n\n587\n\nLink to\nBrunnermeier–Pedersen\n\nWhy this matters for climate transition risk. A transition shock can hit many banks at once (a large p) and be large in size (a large ε). That places the system in the second regime, where more interconnection may amplify rather than absorb the shock. This is an inference for your thesis, not a claim the paper makes.\n\n4. Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009), Market Liquidity and Funding\nLiquidity\nReview of Financial Studies 22(6), 2201–2238 (final version). File note: your PDF is the Advance Access version (published online 10 December 2008), numbered 1–38. Page numbers below are that PDF's own pages. The final journal pages should run about 2200 higher (page 1 = 2201), but I could not check that, so confirm against the journal version before citing."},{"pdf":130,"printed":115,"ch":5,"text":"The one-line message. Traders can only hold positions if they can fund them (margins and haircuts), and funding depends on how liquid the market is. This two-way link creates liquidity spirals: a small funding shock can make markets suddenly illiquid, in many assets at once. ”We provide a model that links an asset's market liquidity (i.e., the ease with which it is traded) and traders' funding liquidity (i.e., the ease with which they can obtain funding)… margins are destabilizing and market liquidity and funding liquidity are mutually reinforcing, leading to liquidity spirals.” (p. 1, abstract) [QUOTE: core claim]\n\n4.1 Core definitions (pp. 1–3)\n• Market liquidity = how easily an asset can be traded. In the model, it is measured by how far the price sits from fundamental value (p. 3).\n• Funding liquidity = how easily traders can get funding. In the model, it is the speculators' scarcity or shadow cost of capital (p. 3).\n• Margin or haircut = price minus collateral value; ”must be financed with the trader's own capital” (p. 1). ”The total margin on all positions cannot exceed a trader's capital at any time” (p. 1).\n\n4.2 The model in plain terms (pp. 7–10)\nTable 5.10. Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009): 4.2 The model in plain terms (pp. 7–10) Element\n\nSetup\n\nTime\n\nt = 0, 1, 2, 3; securities pay off at t = 3; fundamental volatility follows an ARCH process (bad shocks raise future volatility)\n\nCustomers\n\nThree risk-averse agents with offsetting endowment shocks, arriving one at a\ntime. That creates a temporary order imbalance\n\nSpeculators\n\nRisk-neutral dealers or hedge funds that absorb the imbalance (provide\nimmediacy), subject to a margin constraint: total margin ≤ capital (eq. 4, p. 8)\n\nFinanciers\n\nSet margins to cover a value-at-risk at probability π (about 1%) (eqs. 6–7, p. 8–9). They are either informed (know fundamentals) or uninformed (see only prices)\n\nIlliquidity\n\nThe gap between price and fundamental value, |Δ| (eq. 8, p. 10)\n\n4.3 The key results (pp. 10–20)\n1. Proposition 1 (p. 10). Market illiquidity equals the asset's margin times the speculators' shadow cost of funding minus one: |Δ| = m × (φ − 1). So ”a security's market illiquidity is the product of its margin and the shadow cost of funding” (p. 4).\n2. Proposition 2: stabilizing margins (p. 11). If financiers know that prices are temporarily off and will return to fundamentals, margins fall when prices are further from fundamentals (the ”cushioning effect”). This is a benchmark, not the real-world case.\n3. Proposition 3: destabilizing margins (p. 12). If financiers are uninformed and volatility clusters, they read price swings as fundamental risk and raise margins when"},{"pdf":131,"printed":116,"ch":5,"text":"illiquidity rises. This matches real margin-setting, which is ”primarily based on volatility estimates from past price movements”, and it ”introduces a procyclicality that amplifies funding shocks—a major criticism of the Basel II capital regulation” (p. 12). [QUOTE: procyclicality and Basel II]\n4. Proposition 4: fragility (p. 13). Liquidity is ”fragile” if the equilibrium price cannot be continuous in shocks: a tiny change in funding or fundamentals causes a jump in illiquidity. This arises when destabilizing margins or large existing positions (loss effect) make the speculators' demand curve slope upward in price (pp. 13–14).\n• Worked example (pp. 13–16): volatility parameters σ = 10, θ = 0.3, wealth W = 900, VaR π = 1%. Panel A of Figure 3 (p. 14) shows two stable equilibria, a liquid one at price 120 and an illiquid one near 94. With a wealth shock of −150 (panel B) only the illiquid equilibrium survives (p. 15).\n5. Proposition 5: liquidity spirals (p. 17). If the capital constraint is slack, price equals fundamentals and is insensitive to wealth. In an illiquid equilibrium, the price sensitivity to speculator wealth is amplified by two loops:\n• Margin (haircut) spiral: funding shock → less liquidity provision → bigger price swing → higher margins → tighter funding → repeat (pp. 3–4, 18).\n• Loss spiral: funding shock → illiquidity → losses on existing positions → forced selling → further price drop (pp. 3–4).\n• The two together are ”greater than the sum of their separate effects” (p. 18). The algebra: each term of an infinite series is one loop around the circle in Figure 2 (p. 18).\n6. Proposition 6: cross-section (pp. 19–20). (i) Commonality: illiquidities of different securities co-move with each other and with the funding shadow cost. (ii) Commonality of fragility: jumps happen at the same time across assets that speculators trade. (iii) Quality and liquidity: lower-volatility assets are more liquid. (iv) Flight to quality: when funding tightens, the liquidity gap between high-margin and low-margin assets widens (Figure 5, p. 21). ”Paradoxically, liquidity spirals imply that a larger shock to the customers' demand for immediacy leads to a reduction in the provision of immediacy during such times of stress.” (p. 4) [QUOTE: why liquidity vanishes when needed]\n\n4.4 Liquidity risk before the crisis (pp. 22–25)\n• Even when the constraint is not binding at t = 0, the risk that it may bind at t = 1 reduces liquidity provision and lowers prices (p. 22–24).\n• Pricing kernel (eq. 31, p. 23): p0 = E0[p1] + Cov(φ1, p1)/E0[φ1]. Assets that pay little when funding is scarce (φ high) must sell at a discount.\n• Skewness (pp. 24–25): the funding constraint makes speculator returns negatively skewed and prices fat-tailed, because losses are amplified by spirals but gains are not.\n\n4.5 Historical illustrations the paper uses"},{"pdf":132,"printed":117,"ch":5,"text":"Table 5.11. Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009): 4.5 Historical illustrations the paper uses Episode\n\nPoint\n\nPage\n\nMargins on S&P 500 futures Margins rose in each liquidity crisis (Figure 1)\n(1987, 1990, 1998, 2007)\n\n1987 crash\n\n18–19\n\nThirteen NYSE specialist units ”had no buying power”\n(SEC 1988); banks raised haircuts, e.g. 20% to 25%, and\nmade intraday margin calls\n\nRussia default / LTCM 1998 A ”trivial wealth shock relative to global arbitrage capital” had a large effect; LTCM was taken over by fourteen banks\n\n17, 23\n\n2007–08\n\nSubprime losses of several hundred billion dollars (about 5% 3\nof stock market value) became more than $8 trillion of\nmarket loss once borne by levered institutions\n\nBear Stearns (March 2008)\n\nClients terminated brokerage relationships, a run on the\ninvestment bank\n\n4.6 Links to banks and Basel (Appendix A.2, pp. 30–31)\n• Banks face a funding constraint ”in the spirit of Equation (4)” in stress (p. 30).\n• Basel I mapped onto the model. Capital must exceed 8% of risk-weighted assets, with risk weights 0% (cash, government securities), 50% (mortgage-backed loans) and 100% (other). ”The requirement… corresponds to Equation (4) with margins of 0%, 4%, and 8%, respectively” (p. 30). [QUOTE: capital regulation as a margin]\n• The 1996 amendment let banks use internal VaR models; banks then built off-balancesheet vehicles (SIVs, conduits) (pp. 30–31).\n• Margins and haircuts are ”used interchangeably” (p. 29).\n• SEC net capital rule (a 6⅔% of aggregate indebtedness limit, about 15:1 leverage) works similarly (p. 31).\n\n4.7 Testable predictions and policy (pp. 25–27)\n• Speculator capital and volatility (VIX) are state variables for liquidity and risk premia; the effect is nonlinear (strong when capital is low).\n• Margins should co-move with illiquidity, and commonality should be higher when funding is tight.\n• Policy (p. 27): ”Central banks can help mitigate market liquidity problems by controlling funding liquidity”, for example by signalling extra funding in a crisis, which loosens margins (the NY Fed did so in 1987).\n\n4.8 Limits when you cite it\n• It models traders and market makers, not retail banks with deposits. The bank link is made only in Appendix A.2 (p. 30).\n• No network of counterparties. Contagion works through prices and margins, not through bilateral debts. That is the complement to Eisenberg–Noe and Acemoglu et al.\n• One security in the main example; the two-security case is an extension (pp. 21–22)."},{"pdf":133,"printed":118,"ch":5,"text":"• Parameters and figures are illustrative, not calibrated. Several claims (fat tails, positive margins at t = 0) are shown numerically with simulations ”available upon request” (p. 25, fn 12).\n• Final-journal pagination differs (see the file note).\n\n4.9 Quotables from this paper\nTable 5.12. Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009): 4.9 Quotables from this paper\n\nQuote or point\n\nPage\n\nUse in thesis\n\nAbstract: market and funding liquidity are mutually\nreinforcing; liquidity spirals\n\nDefinition and framing\n\nTotal margin cannot exceed capital\n\nFunding-constraint\nlogic\n\nIlliquidity = margin × (shadow cost of funding − 1),\nProp. 1\n\n4, 10\n\nFormal link\n\nMargin spiral and loss spiral (Figure 2)\n\n3–4, 18\n\nFire-sale and haircut\nchannel\n\nMargins based on past volatility ”introduces a\nprocyclicality… a major criticism of the Basel II\ncapital regulation”\n\nProcyclicality of\ncapital rules\n\nFragility: a small shock causes a jump in illiquidity\n(Prop. 4)\n\nNon-linear stress\n\n”Paradoxically… a larger shock… leads to a reduction\nin the provision of immediacy”\n\nWhy liquidity vanishes\nin stress\n\nBasel 8% capital requirement is equivalent to margins\nof 0%, 4%, 8%\n\nBridge to Layer 3\n\nCentral banks can mitigate by controlling funding\nliquidity\n\nPolicy\n\nWhy this matters for the thesis. If transition risk lowers collateral values of carbonintensive assets, lenders may raise haircuts on them, which tightens funding and forces sales (a margin spiral). This is my inference from the paper's mechanism, not a claim the authors make.\n\n5. Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis\nPage numbers: Allen–Gale = JPE pp. 1–33; Eisenberg–Noe = Management Science pp. 236– 249 (OCR-read); Acemoglu et al. = AER pp. 564–608; Brunnermeier–Pedersen = Advance Access PDF pp. 1–38 (see the file note in section 4).\n\n5.1 Ten quotes or points to reach for first"},{"pdf":134,"printed":119,"ch":5,"text":"Table 5.13. Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis: 5.1 Ten quotes or points to reach for first\n\nPoint\n\nPaper and page\n\nA small shock in one region can spread\nAllen–Gale, p. 1\nthrough the whole economy; contagion depends\non how complete the claims structure is\n\nCross-holdings cannot raise total liquidity;\nAllen–Gale, pp. 4–5 Why interbank links\nmutual liquidation ”merely denies liquidity” to\namplify\nthe troubled region\n\n”Contagion depends on the endogenous\npattern of financial claims”\n\nAllen–Gale, p. 24\n\nStructure, not size\n\nA central bank can be seen as completing\nmarkets\n\nAllen–Gale, p. 32\n\nLender-of-last-resort\nargument\n\nEach node pays ”the minimum of what it has\nEisenberg–Noe, pp.\nand what it owes”; clearing vector exists and is 240–242\nunique under regularity\n\nMethod for any\nnetwork stress test\n\nThe round in which a bank defaults measures\nits systemic exposure (fictitious default\nalgorithm, at most n rounds)\n\nEisenberg–Noe, pp.\n243–244\n\nDefault-wave stress\ntesting\n\nDense networks stabilise small shocks but\npropagate large ones: the phase transition\n\nAcemoglu et al., pp. Framing for climate\n564, 578\nshocks of uncertain\nsize\n\nExposure limits designed for small shocks\n”may be counterproductive if the shocks are in\nfact large”\n\nAcemoglu et al., p.\n587\n\nIlliquidity = margin × (shadow cost of funding Brunnermeier–\n− 1); margin and loss spirals\nPedersen, pp. 3–4,\n\nMargins based on past volatility create\nprocyclicality, ”a major criticism of the Basel\nII capital regulation”; the Basel 8% rule maps\nto margins of 0%, 4% and 8%\n\nBrunnermeier–\nPedersen, pp. 12,\n\nWhere it helps\nDefining contagion\n\nPolicy design\n\nFire-sale and haircut\nchannel\nCapital rules and\nprocyclicality\n\n5.2 Four papers, four contagion channels\nTable 5.14. Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis: 5.2 Four papers, four contagion channels Paper\n\nChannel\n\nLink type\n\nShock that matters\n\nMain takeaway\n\nAllen–Gale\n(2000)\n\nLiquidity\n\nInterbank\ndeposits\n\nAggregate liquidity\nshortage\n\nChain structure spreads\ncontagion; complete and\ndisconnected contain it\n\nEisenberg–Noe\n(2001)\n\nSolvency\n(payments)\n\nInterbank\ndebts, any\nnetwork\n\nAny fall in outside\ncash flows\n\nClearing vector exists and is\nfound in at most n rounds\n\nAcemoglu et al.\n(2015)\n\nSolvency\nand\nliquidation\n\nUnsecured\ninterbank\nloans\n\nSize and number of\nshocks\n\nPhase transition: dense is safe\nonly for small shocks"},{"pdf":135,"printed":120,"ch":5,"text":"Paper\n\nChannel\n\nLink type\n\nShock that matters\n\nMain takeaway\n\nBrunnermeier–\nPedersen (2009)\n\nMarket and\nfunding\nliquidity\n\nPrices,\nmargins,\nhaircuts\n\nFunding or capital\nshock to liquidity\nproviders\n\nSpirals; fragility; commonality\nacross assets\n\nAcemoglu et al. say themselves that fire sales and withdrawal of liquidity are ”open question[s] for future research” (p. 587). Brunnermeier–Pedersen is the paper that studies them. Together the four cover both direct (counterparty) and indirect (price and funding) channels.\n\n5.3 How Layer 4 connects to the other layers\n• Layer 3 (capital). The capital stack is the buffer in these models: Allen–Gale's buffer b(ω) (p. 19), Acemoglu et al.'s ”excess liquidity” a − v (p. 574) and Eisenberg–Noe's equity (p. 239). Brunnermeier–Pedersen show that a capital rule such as Basel's 8% acts like a margin (p. 30). Borio et al. (Layer 3) made the ”tsunami, not dominoes” point; Layer 4 supplies the formal domino model.\n• Layer 6 (climate). Climate transition shocks hit many banks together (large p) and can be large (large ε), which in Acemoglu et al. is the regime where dense linkages amplify losses (pp. 578, 582–583). Brunnermeier–Pedersen's margin spiral gives a channel through which higher haircuts on high-carbon collateral could force sales. Both are inferences for your thesis; none of these four papers mentions climate.\n• Layer 5 (empirics). Eisenberg–Noe warn that changes in total asset values can mislead for interconnected firms (pp. 237–238), which matters if you run event studies on bank returns.\n\n5.4 What I read, and what I did not\nTable 5.15. Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis: 5.4 What I read, and what I did not Paper\n\nRead\n\nNot read in detail\n\nAllen–Gale\n\nAll 33 pages\n\nNothing skipped; references only skimmed\n\nEisenberg–Noe\n\nAll 14 pages, via OCR Appendix 1 proof (p. 248–249) checked for\nstructure only\n\nAcemoglu et al.\n\nPages 564–588 (all\nmain text)\n\nAppendix A (harmonic distance) read only to its\nfirst result, Appendix B proofs (to p. 608) and the\nonline appendix not read\n\nBrunnermeier–Pedersen\n\nPages 1–33 (text,\nappendices A and B)\n\nRest of the proofs of Proposition 6 and references\n\nFlags: (1) Eisenberg–Noe has a damaged text layer, so quotes came from OCR; (2) Brunnermeier–Pedersen is the Advance Access PDF, so journal page numbers may differ; (3) the few formulae in my notes (for example harmonic distance and the shock thresholds) were written from the text, so check the symbols against the paper before you write equations into the thesis."},{"pdf":136,"printed":121,"ch":5,"text":"5.5 Suggested use in the thesis\n1. In the literature review, present the four papers as channels (liquidity, payments, network structure, funding), not as four separate stories.\n2. In the theory chapter, use the Acemoglu et al. phase-transition result to argue that the effect of interconnection on climate stress is conditional on the shock size.\n3. In the Indian context, state clearly that none of these papers tests Indian banks. The link is conceptual, and any claim about public sector banks needs evidence from your own data (Layer 3 sources, RBI reports)."},{"pdf":137,"printed":122,"ch":5,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 4"},{"pdf":138,"printed":123,"ch":5,"text":""},{"pdf":139,"printed":124,"ch":5,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 4"},{"pdf":140,"printed":125,"ch":5,"text":""}],"cards":[]};

window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.ln=window.CHTML.ln||{};window.CHTML.ln[5]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Before you start</h1>\n<p data-b=\"2\">Layer 4 is about how one bank’ s trouble reaches others. You can read it on its own, but it helps to know the five ideas below and to have done Layer 3 (capital) first. The papers are theoretical and mathematical; these notes give the logic and keep the algebra light.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">Concepts you need</h2>\n<table data-b=\"4\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.1.</strong> Before you start: Concepts you need</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Concept</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Plain meaning</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it appears</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Interbank exposure</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">One bank lends to, or holds a deposit in, another; a loss at the borrower is a loss for the lender</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">All four papers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Liquidity vs solvency</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Illiquid: cannot pay <em>now</em>. Insolvent: assets worth less than debts. A bank can be one without the other</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale (liquidity); Eisenberg–Noe, Acemoglu et al. (solvency)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Seniority and pro-rata payment</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Senior creditors (for example depositors) are paid first; junior creditors share what is left in proportion to their claims</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eisenberg–Noe, Acemoglu et al.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fixed point / clearing</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Everyone’ s payment depends on everyone else’ s, so you solve for the set of payments that is consistent with all of them</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eisenberg–Noe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Margin, haircut, fire sale</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Margin or haircut = the part of an asset’ s price you cannot borrow against; a fire sale is a forced sale at a low price</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Brunnermeier–Pedersen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Network shapes</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ring</strong> (each bank owes one other), <strong>complete</strong> (each owes all others equally), <strong>disconnected or clustered</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale, Acemoglu et al.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"5\">A basic grasp of a deposit contract (promise to pay c1 on demand) helps for Allen–Gale. If you did Layer 3, you already know capital as a buffer and Basel’ s risk weights, which Brunnermeier–Pedersen use in Appendix A.2.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"6\" id=\"b6\">Map of the four papers</h2>\n<table data-b=\"7\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.2.</strong> Before you start: Map of the four papers</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Paper</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Question</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Type of link</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Math level</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale (2000)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Do banks that insure each other against local liquidity shocks make the system fragile?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Interbank deposits</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Moderate; mostly logic and diagrams</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eisenberg–Noe (2001)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Given a web of debts, what does each bank actually pay?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Interbank debts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Higher; fixed-point and matrix algebra</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acemoglu et al. (2015)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Which network shapes handle small and large shocks best?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unsecured interbank loans</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Higher; proofs in appendix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Brunnermeier–Pedersen (2009)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">How do funding limits and market liquidity feed each other?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Prices, margins, haircuts (no direct links)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Moderate; intuition-led with numerical examples</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"8\" id=\"b8\">Suggested reading order</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"9\"><strong>Allen–Gale</strong> first: the cleanest story of contagion, and the others refer to it.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"10\"><strong>Eisenberg–Noe</strong>: the payment-clearing engine behind network stress tests.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"11\"><strong>Acemoglu et al.</strong>: builds on both; it explains when density helps and when it hurts.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"12\"><strong>Brunnermeier–Pedersen</strong>: a different channel (prices and funding). It can be read any time after Allen–Gale.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"13\">If time is short, read sections 1 and 3 of these notes first, then the master quotables in section 5.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"14\" id=\"b14\">File and page notes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"15\">Eisenberg–Noe’ s PDF has a damaged text layer; I used OCR, so check quotes against the page.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"16\">Brunnermeier–Pedersen is the Advance Access version (pages 1–38), so confirm journal page numbers before citing.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"17\">Allen–Gale and Acemoglu et al. are the printed journal pages.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-b=\"18\" id=\"b18\">1. Allen and Gale (2000), Financial Contagion</h1>\n<p data-b=\"19\"><em>Journal of Political Economy</em> 108(1), 1–33. Page numbers below are the printed journal pages (1–33).</p>\n<p data-b=\"20\"><strong>The one-line message.</strong> Banks insure each other against local liquidity shocks by holding each other’ s deposits. That works perfectly in normal times, but if the economy as a whole runs short of liquidity, the same cross-holdings carry a small shock from one region to every other region. Whether this happens depends on the <em>pattern</em> of the claims, not only on their size.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"21\" id=\"b21\">1.1 The question and the answer (pp. 1–6)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"22\">Earlier bank-run theory (Diamond–Dybvig) treats one bank. Allen–Gale ask how a crisis passes <em>between</em> banks when the only link is financial claims.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"23\">They use a stylised model with no panics, no sunspots and no information problems. Contagion is shown as the <strong>only possible outcome</strong> (every continuation equilibrium), not one of several.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"24\">Their finding in one sentence: contagion needs (i) an <em>aggregate</em> liquidity shortage, and (ii) an interbank market that is <em>incomplete but connected</em>.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"25\">\"A small liquidity preference shock in one region can spread by contagion throughout the economy. The possibility of contagion depends strongly on the completeness of the structure of interregional claims. Complete claims structures are shown to be more robust than incomplete structures.\" (p. 1) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: the thesis-ready summary<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"26\">Two points from the introduction worth keeping (pp. 4–5): cross-holdings of deposits \"cannot increase the total amount of liquidity\" in the system, and when banks liquidate claims on each other at the same moment this \"merely denies liquidity\" to the troubled region. The paper also gives a free-rider reason: each bank prefers to protect its own long asset and let others bear the liquidation.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"27\" id=\"b27\">1.2 The model (pp. 6–10)</h2>\n<table data-b=\"28\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.3.</strong> Allen and Gale (2000): 1.2 The model (pp. 6–10)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Element</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Setup</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dates</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0 (invest), 1 (early withdrawals), 2 (late withdrawals)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Assets</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Short asset</strong>: 1 unit today gives 1 unit tomorrow. <strong>Long asset</strong>: 1 unit today gives R &gt; 1 at date 2, but only r &lt; 1 if liquidated at date 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Regions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Four, A, B, C, D, each with many identical banks and consumers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Shocks</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A consumer is \"early\" (needs money at date 1) or \"late\". The early fraction is ωH in a high region and ωL in a low one. A and C are high when B and D are low, and vice versa (Table 1). The average across regions is a constant γ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Contract</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks offer a demand deposit paying c1 at date 1 or c2 at date 2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"29\">Key logic. Because the regional shocks are perfectly negatively correlated, the <em>economy</em> has no aggregate uncertainty, even though each region does. A planner can therefore insure every region fully (Proposition 1, p. 9: the first-best is also incentive-efficient, so late consumers do not run).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"30\" id=\"b30\">1.3 How banks decentralise risk sharing (pp. 10–15)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"31\">Banks do it by holding <strong>deposits in other regions’ banks</strong> (interbank deposits). A region short of liquidity withdraws from a region that has a surplus. Two structures achieve the same first-best:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"32\"><strong>Complete market (Fig. 1).</strong> Each bank holds z = (ωH − γ)/2 in <em>each</em> of the other regions.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"33\"><strong>Incomplete market (Fig. 2).</strong> Each bank holds z = ωH − γ in <em>one</em> neighbour only, in a chain A→B→C→D→A.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"34\"><strong>Disconnected incomplete (Fig. 3).</strong> A and B hold each other’ s deposits, and C and D hold each other’ s. This also gets the first-best (p. 14–15).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"35\">In normal states (S1, S2) all three look the same. Their fragility differs sharply. The authors stress this: the structures \"turn out to have striking differences for financial fragility\" (p. 15).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"36\" id=\"b36\">1.4 The fragility experiment (pp. 15–22)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"37\">The authors add a state S with probability zero in which total demand for liquidity is slightly higher: region A has γ + ε early consumers, the other three have γ (Table 2, p. 16). Total liquidity is now short.</p>\n<p data-b=\"38\"><strong>Pecking order (p. 17).</strong> Bank-by-bank, the cheapest source of cash at date 1 is the short asset (cost 1), then liquidating interbank deposits (cost c2/c1), and last the long asset (cost R/r). So 1 &lt; c2/c1 &lt; R/r. Banks always sell short assets first, then deposits, and the long asset only last.</p>\n<p data-b=\"39\"><strong>Bank states (p. 17).</strong> <em>Solvent</em>: pays everyone from short assets and deposits. <em>Insolvent</em>: must sell some long asset. <em>Bankrupt</em>: cannot pay even by selling everything.</p>\n<p data-b=\"40\"><strong>Buffer (p. 19).</strong> The buffer b(ω) = r<span>[</span>x − (1 − ω)c1/R<span>]</span> is how much cash a bank can raise from the long asset without making late consumers run (they must still be promised c1 at date 2).</p>\n<p data-b=\"41\"><strong>The contagion chain (pp. 19–22).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"42\">If ε·c1 is within region A’ s buffer (condition 8), A is insolvent but nobody else is hurt.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"43\">If ε·c1 exceeds the buffer (condition 9), A is bankrupt. Every bank wants to pull its deposits from every other bank at once, so mutual withdrawals net to zero and no region can import liquidity.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"44\">Because A is bankrupt, a deposit in A is worth q_A &lt; c1. Region D holds z of them, so it loses at least z(c1 − q_A).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"45\">If that loss exceeds D’ s buffer (condition 11), D is bankrupt too, then C, then B. The spillover grows with each failure.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"46\"><strong>Proposition 2 (p. 21).</strong> With the chain structure of Fig. 2, a zero-probability state S, and conditions (9) and (11), \"the banks in all regions must go bankrupt at date 1 in state S.\" <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: formal contagion result<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"47\">Low r makes this worse twice: the loss on A’ s liquidated assets is bigger <em>and</em> every buffer is smaller (p. 20).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"48\" id=\"b48\">1.5 Why completeness helps, and why disconnection helps too (pp. 22–24)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"49\"><strong>Robustness (Sec. VI, pp. 22–23).</strong> With the complete structure each bank holds z/2 in each of three regions. The loss on any one failed region is (z/2)(c1 − q_A*), which can fit inside the buffer (condition 13) even when conditions (8) and (11) fail. The exposure to any single bank is smaller, so the shock is shared out.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"50\"><strong>Containment (Sec. VII, pp. 23–24).</strong> In the disconnected structure (Fig. 3) a shock in A may hit B but \"there is no reason why <span>[</span>it<span>]</span> should spread any further\" (p. 23).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"51\"><strong>Non-monotone result (p. 24).</strong> Crisis is confined to A with complete markets, hits all four regions with the chain, and hits only A and B when disconnected.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"52\">\"Contagion depends on the endogenous pattern of financial claims.\" (p. 24) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: structure, not size, drives contagion<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"53\">Note the caveat (pp. 24): complete markets also have equilibria with contagion, because banks can <em>choose</em> overlapping claims. The chain of Fig. 2 matters because it is the <em>only</em> pattern that market structure allows.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"54\" id=\"b54\">1.6 Could other interbank instruments avoid it? (pp. 24–27)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"55\"><strong>Contingent or non-demandable claims (pp. 25–26).</strong> No. If banks may refuse to pay, they will do so whenever the alternative is selling the long asset, which has the same effect as netting claims. The paper says the use of demand deposits in the interbank market \"is not analogous to\" their use in the retail market (p. 25). Suspension of convertibility prevents runs in retail but not in the interbank case.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"56\"><strong>Ex post lending (pp. 26–27).</strong> An interbank loan market at date 1 stops <em>spillover</em> but cannot cure the region in trouble. With an economy-wide cash shortage the loan rate would be R/r, which is too high to help A. Ex post markets also face lemons and hold-up problems, and cannot share asset-return risk.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"57\" id=\"b57\">1.7 Discussion and extensions (pp. 27–32)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"58\"><strong>Equilibrium and the planner (pp. 27–29).</strong> Making the shock symmetric (any region can be hit with probability δ, Table 3) does not change things for small δ. Even a planner choosing date-0 portfolios, but working through demand deposits, allows contagion because insuring against rare shocks means holding more of the low-return liquid asset, which is not worth it. So contagion can be \"constrained efficient\" (p. 29).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"59\"><strong>Many regions (pp. 29–30).</strong> With complete markets, adding regions makes a single shock negligible: the system is robust. With incomplete markets, a single small shock can spread to an arbitrarily large number of regions.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"60\"><strong>Sunspots and risky assets (p. 31).</strong> A multiple-equilibria reading is possible but not needed. Holding claims on <em>banks</em> is much more dangerous than holding the <em>assets</em> directly: a bank lending directly into another region’ s long asset can wait until date 2 and earn R, while interbank claims get marked down to the liquidation value.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"61\"><strong>Central bank (p. 32).</strong> \"One way of thinking about the role of a central bank is to complete markets\": every bank has a link to it, so the central bank can stop inefficient liquidation.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"62\" id=\"b62\">1.8 Limits to remember when you cite this paper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"63\">It is a <strong>liquidity</strong> model, not a solvency model: banks fail because depositors want cash, not because assets lose value (the extension to risky assets is only sketched, p. 31).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"64\">The contagion state S has probability zero, and results for small positive δ are argued, not fully proved (p. 28).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"65\">Regions are symmetric and only four; the ’ many regions’ result is an extension (pp. 29–30).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"66\">No capital, no leverage, no regulation. This is why Layers 3 (capital) and 4 (networks) need to be read together.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"67\" id=\"b67\">1.9 Quotables from this paper</h2>\n<table data-b=\"68\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.4.</strong> Allen and Gale (2000): 1.9 Quotables from this paper</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote or point</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Use in thesis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"A small liquidity preference shock in one region can spread by contagion throughout the economy…\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Core definition of contagion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cross-holdings cannot increase total liquidity; mutual liquidation merely denies liquidity to the troubled region</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4–5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why interbank links can amplify</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pecking order: short asset, then deposits, then long asset (1 &lt; c2/c1 &lt; R/r)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">17</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fire-sale logic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Proposition 2: all regions go bankrupt in state S</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">21</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Formal result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Contagion depends on the endogenous pattern of financial claims</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Network structure matters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Demand deposits in interbank markets are not analogous to retail deposits</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">25</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why suspension doesn’ t work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Complete markets: more regions make a single shock negligible; incomplete markets can propagate to arbitrarily many regions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Link to Acemoglu et al. (2015)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A central bank can be thought of as completing markets</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">32</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Policy link (lender of last resort, RBI)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"69\"><strong>Bridge to the next paper.</strong> Allen–Gale show contagion with four symmetric regions and a single type of claim. Eisenberg–Noe (next) give a general method for any network of debts: how much each bank can actually pay when everyone owes everyone.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"70\" id=\"b70\">2. Eisenberg and Noe (2001), Systemic Risk in Financial Systems</h1>\n<p data-b=\"71\"><em>Management Science</em> 47(2), 236–249. Page numbers are the printed pages. <strong>File note:</strong> the PDF in your folder has a broken text layer, so I read it by OCR (page images). Wording of quotes was taken from the OCR text; check against the page before quoting verbatim.</p>\n<p data-b=\"72\"><strong>The one-line message.</strong> When firms owe each other money in loops (A owes B, B owes C, C owes A), you cannot read off who pays what. Eisenberg–Noe prove that a consistent set of payments, the <strong>clearing payment vector</strong>, always exists, is usually unique, and can be found by a simple round-by-round algorithm that also ranks firms by how exposed they are to others’ defaults.</p>\n<p data-b=\"73\">This is the mathematical engine behind Acemoglu et al. (2015). Their \"payment equilibrium\" generalises it (Acemoglu et al., p. 571).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"74\" id=\"b74\">2.1 Problem and contribution (pp. 236–238)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"75\">Firms’ values depend on what other firms pay them, which depends on still other firms. The authors call this <strong>cyclical interdependence</strong>: \"A default by Firm A on its obligations to Firm B may lead B to default on its obligations to C. A default by C may, in turn, have a feedback effect on A.\" (p. 236)</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"76\">Real settings: bank payment systems (CHIPS, Fedwire), clearing houses. Historical failures cited include Herstatt (1974), the Bank of New York overnight shortfall of $22.6 billion (1985), the US S&amp;L bailout (about $500 billion), LTCM, and the 1982 al-Manakh market collapse in Kuwait, where gross mutual liabilities exceeded four times Kuwait’ s GDP (pp. 236–237).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"77\">The authors say that, to their knowledge, theirs is \"the first to analyze, in a general fashion, the properties of intercorporate cash flows in financial systems featuring cyclical interdependence\" (p. 237).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"78\" id=\"b78\">2.2 The model (pp. 238–240)</h2>\n<table data-b=\"79\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.5.</strong> Eisenberg and Noe (2001): 2.2 The model (pp. 238–240)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Object</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">n nodes</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Firms or banks in one clearing system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">L (n × n)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nominal liabilities: L_ij = what node i owes node j. Non-negative, zero diagonal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">e</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Operating cash flow from outside the system, e ≥ 0 for each node</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p̄_i</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Total obligation of node i: sum of L_ij over j</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Π</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Relative liabilities matrix: Π_ij = L_ij / p̄_i, so rows sum to 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Vector of actual total payments by each node</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"80\">Inflow to node i = (sum over j of Π_ji p_j) + e_i. Equity of node i = inflow minus payment, floored at zero (p. 239).</p>\n<p data-b=\"81\"><strong>Three rules of a clearing vector (Definition 1, p. 239).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"82\"><strong>Limited liability.</strong> A node never pays more than it has.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"83\"><strong>Absolute priority.</strong> Debt is paid before equity: either the node pays its obligation in full, or equity is zero (all value goes to creditors).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"84\"><strong>Proportionality.</strong> If a node defaults, all creditor nodes are paid pro rata to their nominal claim (equal priority).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"85\"><strong>Fixed-point form (p. 240).</strong> Each node pays \"the minimum of what it has and what it owes\": p = Φ(p) = (Πᵀp + e) ∧ p̄. A clearing vector is a fixed point of Φ.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"86\" id=\"b86\">2.3 Main results (pp. 240–244)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"87\"><strong>Existence (Theorem 1, p. 240).</strong> Φ is increasing, concave and nonexpansive, so by Tarski’ s fixed-point theorem a greatest and a least clearing vector always exist. <strong>Equity at each node is the same under all clearing vectors.</strong></p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"88\"><strong>Uniqueness (pp. 241–242).</strong> Not guaranteed in general (an example is in Appendix 2, p. 249). It holds under <strong>regularity</strong>: every node’ s <em>risk orbit</em> (all nodes reachable by some chain of debts) contains a node with positive operating cash flow, so that value from somewhere can reach every part of the system. Theorem 2 (p. 242): in a regular system the greatest and least clearing vectors coincide.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"89\"><strong>Fictitious default algorithm (pp. 243–244).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"90\">Round 1: assume everyone pays in full; find who cannot (first-order defaults).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"91\">Round 2: recompute payments assuming only those nodes default; find new defaults (second-order).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"92\">Repeat. Lemma 3 (p. 243): this converges to the clearing vector in at most n steps.</p></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"93\"><strong>Systemic-risk ranking (p. 244).</strong> The round in which a node defaults is a measure of its exposure: first-round defaults are \"fundamentally insolvent\"; later waves are nodes that fail only because others failed.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"94\"><strong>Programming characterisation (p. 244).</strong> Maximising any strictly increasing function of payments subject to limited liability gives the clearing vector (Lemma 4). The authors note that Kuwait’ s authorities used this approach to clear the al-Manakh debts (p. 245).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"95\" id=\"b95\">2.4 Comparative statics (pp. 245–247)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"96\"><strong>Lemma 5 (p. 245).</strong> The clearing vector is a concave, increasing, nonexpansive function of operating cash flows and of nominal liabilities. Concave means that adding a unit of cash helps less than losing a unit hurts.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"97\"><strong>Consequence 1:</strong> more volatile cash flows (in the second-order-dominance sense) reduce expected debt payments (p. 245–246).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"98\"><strong>Consequence 2: total system value falls with volatility</strong> even with no bankruptcy costs. The Corollary (p. 246): \"Increases in the volatility… of operating cash flows lowers the total value of all nodes in the system.\"</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"99\"><strong>Equity is ambiguous (p. 246).</strong> Higher risk lowers payments <em>to</em> a node (bad for net creditors) and payments <em>by</em> it (good for net debtors). So some firms’ equity can fall even though the total is unchanged.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"100\"><strong>Lemma 6 (p. 247).</strong> A mixture of two systems cannot do better than the better one or worse than the worse one on payments; a stronger concavity result fails.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"101\">The authors conclude that \"using changes in total asset values to measure the effect of an economic shock on a group of tightly interconnected companies (e.g., Japanese banks) can be highly misleading.\" (p. 238) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: why firm-by-firm event studies can mislead<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"102\" id=\"b102\">2.5 Extensions the authors suggest (pp. 247–248)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"103\">Valuing debt and equity and computing default probabilities, default correlation and value-at-risk with stochastic cash flows (p. 247).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"104\">Cross-holdings of equity; several priority classes; violations of absolute priority.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"105\">Dynamics: nodes that default become owned by creditors; a \"central bank\" lends at a market rate. They call this the hardest extension (p. 248).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"106\" id=\"b106\">2.6 Limits when you cite it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"107\"><strong>One clearing date, no liquidation, no fire sales.</strong> There are no bankruptcy costs; value is conserved. Contagion here is purely redistribution of fixed value. Acemoglu et al. add costly liquidation and outside senior claims.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"108\"><strong>Network and cash flows are given</strong> (p. 239). The paper explains why firms would still pay out ex post: ex ante contracts are assumed perfectly enforceable (p. 240).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"109\"><strong>Uniqueness needs regularity.</strong> Without it, many payment vectors can be consistent (App. 2, p. 249).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"110\">It is a <strong>computation and existence</strong> paper; it makes no claim about which network shapes are safer. That is the contribution of Allen–Gale and Acemoglu et al.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"111\" id=\"b111\">2.7 Worked example in words (p. 249, Appendix 2)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"112\">Two banks, each owing the other 1.00, with no outside cash. Any p = t(1,1) for t in <span>[</span>0,1<span>]</span> clears, so payments are indeterminate. Give node 1 one cent of outside cash and the only clearing vector is (1.00, 1.00) because anything less would leave node 1 with equity while not fully paying its debt, which absolute priority forbids.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"113\" id=\"b113\">2.8 Quotables from this paper</h2>\n<table data-b=\"114\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.6.</strong> Eisenberg and Noe (2001): 2.8 Quotables from this paper</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote or point</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Use in thesis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cyclical interdependence: A defaults on B, B on C, and C feeds back to A</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">236</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defines the feedback loop in bank networks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">First general analysis of intercorporate cash flows with cyclical interdependence (the authors’ claim)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">237</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Position in the literature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Clearing vector: three rules (limited liability, absolute priority, proportionality)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">239</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Method for any network simulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Each node pays \"the minimum of what it has and what it owes\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">240</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Intuitive statement of the fixed point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Clearing vector exists; unique under regularity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">240–242</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justify network simulations are well-defined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fictitious default algorithm converges in at most n rounds; default round = exposure to systemic risk</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">243–244</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stress-test method (waves of default)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Volatility lowers total system value even without bankruptcy costs</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">246</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk without dissipation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Total asset value changes can be misleading for interconnected firms</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">237–238</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Caution on event studies of bank returns</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"115\"><strong>Bridge to the next paper.</strong> Eisenberg–Noe take the network as given and find who pays what. Acemoglu et al. (previous section) ask which <em>network shapes</em> make cascades worse. The last paper, Brunnermeier–Pedersen, switches off direct links altogether: banks can be hit through market prices and funding.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"116\" id=\"b116\">3. Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015), Systemic Risk and Stability in Financial Networks</h1>\n<p data-b=\"117\"><em>American Economic Review</em> 105(2), 564–608. Page numbers are the printed AER pages. Main text is pp. 564–588; the rest is appendix and proofs (read for the statements, not the proofs).</p>\n<p data-b=\"118\"><strong>The one-line message.</strong> Dense interbank networks are shock absorbers when shocks are small and shock transmitters when shocks are large. The same structure that makes a system robust can make it fragile. The authors call this a <strong>phase transition</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"119\">\"as long as the magnitude of negative shocks affecting financial institutions are sufficiently small, a more densely connected financial network… enhances financial stability. However, beyond a certain point, dense interconnections serve as a mechanism for the propagation of shocks, leading to a more fragile financial system.\" (p. 564) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: the abstract, thesis-ready<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"120\" id=\"b120\">3.1 Why the paper exists (pp. 564–568)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"121\">After 2008, \"the architecture of the financial system plays a central role in shaping systemic risk\" had become conventional wisdom, but its exact role \"remains, at best, imperfectly understood\" (p. 564).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"122\">Two camps: (a) <strong>Allen–Gale</strong> and Freixas–Parigi–Rochet: more interconnection spreads losses over more creditors, so it is stabilising. (b) Vivier-Lirimont, Blume et al., and others: more counterparties means a higher chance of systemic collapse, like an epidemic (p. 565).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"123\">This paper reconciles the two: both are right, depending on shock size (p. 566).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"124\">It also confirms Haldane’ s (2009) \"robust-yet-fragile\" conjecture: connections act as shock-absorbers within a range, then \"the system <span>[</span>flips to<span>]</span> the wrong side of the knife-edge\" (p. 566).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"125\">A policy footnote (p. 564, fn 2): Dodd-Frank’ s single-counterparty exposure limits were motivated by this view.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"126\" id=\"b126\">3.2 The model (pp. 569–572)</h2>\n<table data-b=\"127\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.7.</strong> Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015): 3.2 The model (pp. 569–572)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Piece</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">n risk-neutral banks, 3 periods (t = 0, 1, 2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Project</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Each bank invests; it pays random z_i at t = 1 and a fixed non-pledgeable A at t = 2. It can be liquidated early at t = 1 for only a fraction ζ &lt; 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Interbank debt</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bank j owes y_ij to bank i (face value). All junior creditors (other banks) have equal seniority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Senior claims</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Each bank also owes an outside obligation v &gt; 0 (retail deposits, wages, taxes, secured creditors) that is paid first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Network</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weighted directed graph of the debts y_ij</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"128\">Because long-run returns are not pledgeable, all debts must be settled at t = 1. A bank that cannot pay liquidates its project (eq. 1, p. 570), and if that is not enough it defaults. Junior creditors are then paid pro rata, but only if the senior claim v is covered (eq. 2, p. 571).</p>\n<p data-b=\"129\"><strong>Payment equilibrium (Definition 2, p. 571).</strong> A set of mutually consistent repayments and liquidations. It generalises the <strong>clearing vector</strong> of Eisenberg–Noe (2001) by adding seniority and costly liquidation (p. 571). <strong>Proposition 1 (p. 572)</strong>: an equilibrium always exists and is generically unique.</p>\n<p data-b=\"130\"><strong>Network types (p. 570).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"131\"><strong>Regular network</strong>: every bank has the same total claims and liabilities y, so differences in fragility come only from <em>who owes whom</em>, not from size or leverage.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"132\"><strong>Ring</strong>: each bank owes only one creditor. The sparsest regular network.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"133\"><strong>Complete</strong>: each bank’ s debt is split equally across the other n − 1 banks. The densest.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"134\"><strong>γ-convex combination</strong> of ring and complete: moves from ring to complete as γ rises (Definition 1, p. 570).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"135\"><strong>Shock setup (p. 572).</strong> Short-term returns are a (normal) or a − ε (shocked). The critical size is ε* = n(a − v), the total <strong>excess liquidity</strong> in the system (p. 574).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"136\" id=\"b136\">3.3 Results, step by step (pp. 573–585)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"137\"><strong>More interbank debt, more fragility (Prop. 3, p. 574).</strong> Scaling all liabilities up by β &gt; 1 makes the system less stable and less resilient, whatever its structure.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"138\">**Small shocks (ε &lt; ε<em>; Prop. 4, p. 574).</em>* The ring is the <em>least</em> stable and resilient network, the complete network the <em>most</em>. The γ-combination gets better as γ rises. Logic: spreading losses over many creditors lets excess liquidity absorb them; in a ring, losses go entirely to one creditor.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"139\"><strong>Majorization (Prop. 5, p. 576).</strong> Any mixing of the distressed bank’ s liabilities into a more even pattern does not increase defaults, for small shocks. <strong>A caution (Example, p. 576):</strong> equalising a bank’ s two exposures can still raise fragility if it starves the bank that needed the resources.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"140\">**Large shocks (ε &gt; ε<em>; Prop. 6, pp. 577–578).</em>* The picture flips: the complete and ring networks are now the <em>least</em> stable. Any δ-connected (weakly linked clusters) network is <em>strictly better</em> than both.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"141\">\"the complete network exhibits a form of phase transition: it flips from being the most to the least stable and resilient network… In particular, when ε &gt; ε*, all banks in the complete network default.\" (p. 578) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: the phase transition in plain words<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"142\"><strong>Two shock absorbers (p. 578).</strong> (i) <em>Excess liquidity</em> of non-distressed banks, used best in dense networks. (ii) <em>Senior creditors’ claims v</em>: in a weakly connected network, outsiders (depositors, taxpayers) absorb some of the loss instead of other banks. When excess liquidity is exhausted, absorber (ii) matters, and it works best in sparse networks.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"143\"><strong>Harmonic distance (Def. 6, Prop. 8, pp. 579–580).</strong> m_ij = 1 + Σ (y_ik / y) m_kj. It equals the mean hitting time of a random walk on the debt network (Prop. A1, p. 588). Banks <em>closer</em> to a distressed bank default first. The bank closest to all others is the \"too-interconnected-to-fail\" bank.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"144\"><strong>Why not eigenvector centrality (p. 580).</strong> Standard centrality measures suit <em>linear</em> interactions. With debt contracts the interactions are non-linear, so harmonic distance, not centrality, identifies systemic importance.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"145\"><strong>Bottleneck parameter φ (Def. 7, Lemma 1, Cor. 2–3, pp. 580–582).</strong> φ is the smallest amount of cross-linkage you must cut to split the network into two roughly equal halves. High φ (dense) means everyone defaults under a large shock; low φ (modular) means someone survives.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"146\"><strong>Multiple shocks (Prop. 9, pp. 582–583).</strong> The threshold becomes ε*_p = n(a − v)/p, falling with the number of shocks p. So <strong>the number of shocks acts like the size of shocks</strong>. A surprise: with several shocks and moderate y, the ring can beat the complete network (part iii).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"147\"><strong>Positive liquidation value (Prop. 10–11, pp. 583–584).</strong> If ζ &gt; 0 and partial liquidation is allowed, the transition gets smoother (an intermediate regime). If liquidation must be full, the sharp transition returns.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"148\"><strong>Size heterogeneity (Prop. 12, p. 585).</strong> Bigger banks are more vulnerable to contagion, in a size-adjusted harmonic distance.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"149\" id=\"b149\">3.4 Policy implications (pp. 586–587)</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"150\">\"imposing limits on the exposure between pairs of financial institutions motivated by the possibility of small shocks may be counterproductive if the shocks are in fact large.\" (p. 587) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: regulation depends on shock size<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"151\">Ex ante: regulating the extent of interbank linkage must be informed by the expected size of shocks.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"152\">Ex post: injecting funds into, or bailing out, the systemically important (closest by harmonic distance) institutions contains contagion, though with moral hazard (p. 587).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"153\">Working-paper result (p. 587): with more than three banks, a network-formation <strong>externality</strong> appears: borrowers do not internalise the effect on their creditors’ creditors, so observed networks can be inefficient.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"154\" id=\"b154\">3.5 Limits and what the authors say is missing (pp. 587–588)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"155\">Only <strong>counterparty risk via unsecured debt</strong>. Fire sales on common assets and withdrawal of liquidity (repo roll-over, haircuts) are named as open problems (p. 587). Those are precisely Brunnermeier–Pedersen’ s topic.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"156\">No maturity mismatch, no repos, and no distinction between bank types (p. 587–588).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"157\">The network is <strong>given</strong>, not chosen. There is no empirical test; the authors call a systematic empirical investigation \"an important area for research\" (p. 588).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"158\">Stylised: n banks, two shock values, regular networks. The paper’ s own footnote says it uses the word \"bank\" for ease only (p. 565, fn 3).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"159\" id=\"b159\">3.6 Reading it against Allen–Gale</h2>\n<table data-b=\"160\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.8.</strong> Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015): 3.6 Reading it against Allen–Gale</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale (2000)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Acemoglu et al. (2015)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Link</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Interbank deposits (liquidity insurance)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unsecured interbank loans</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Mechanism</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Liquidity shortage, fire sale of long asset</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Counterparty default, seniority, costly liquidation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Structures</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4 regions: complete, chain, disconnected</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Any network: ring, complete, δ-connected, general</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Key variable</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Completeness and connectedness</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Shock size (and number) relative to excess liquidity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Verdict</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Complete is robust, chain fragile, disconnected contains</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Complete is robust only for small shocks; modular is best for large ones</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"161\">Acemoglu et al. explicitly say Allen–Gale \"also note that compared to a four-bank ring network, a pairwise-connected… network can be less prone to financial contagion\" but did not study shock size or the phase transition (p. 567, fn 5).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"162\" id=\"b162\">3.7 Quotables from this paper</h2>\n<table data-b=\"163\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.9.</strong> Acemoglu, Ozdaglar and Tahbaz-Salehi (2015): 3.7 Quotables from this paper</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote or point</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Use in thesis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Abstract: dense networks stabilise small shocks but propagate large ones</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">564</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Core framing for climate shocks of unknown size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Role of architecture \"remains, at best, imperfectly understood\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">564</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justify network analysis of banks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Robust-yet-fragile (Haldane 2009)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">566, 579</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Policy language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Complete network flips from most to least stable</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">578</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Phase transition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Two shock absorbers: excess liquidity and senior creditors</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">578</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why size matters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Harmonic distance beats off-the-shelf centrality for systemic importance</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">580</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">If you use network measures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Number of shocks acts like shock size (ε*_p = n(a − v)/p)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">582–583</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Many banks hit by climate events at once</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Exposure limits for small shocks can be counterproductive for large ones</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">587</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Regulation design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fire sales and liquidity withdrawal left for future research</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">587</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Link to Brunnermeier–Pedersen</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"164\"><strong>Why this matters for climate transition risk.</strong> A transition shock can hit many banks at once (a large p) and be large in size (a large ε). That places the system in the second regime, where more interconnection may amplify rather than absorb the shock. This is an inference for your thesis, not a claim the paper makes.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"165\" id=\"b165\">4. Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009), Market Liquidity and Funding Liquidity</h1>\n<p data-b=\"166\"><em>Review of Financial Studies</em> 22(6), 2201–2238 (final version). <strong>File note:</strong> your PDF is the <em>Advance Access</em> version (published online 10 December 2008), numbered 1–38. Page numbers below are that PDF’ s own pages. The final journal pages should run about 2200 higher (page 1 = 2201), but I could not check that, so confirm against the journal version before citing.</p>\n<p data-b=\"167\"><strong>The one-line message.</strong> Traders can only hold positions if they can fund them (margins and haircuts), and funding depends on how liquid the market is. This two-way link creates <strong>liquidity spirals</strong>: a small funding shock can make markets suddenly illiquid, in many assets at once.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"168\">\"We provide a model that links an asset’ s market liquidity (i.e., the ease with which it is traded) and traders’ funding liquidity (i.e., the ease with which they can obtain funding)… margins are destabilizing and market liquidity and funding liquidity are mutually reinforcing, leading to liquidity spirals.\" (p. 1, abstract) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: core claim<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"169\" id=\"b169\">4.1 Core definitions (pp. 1–3)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"170\"><strong>Market liquidity</strong> = how easily an asset can be traded. In the model, it is measured by how far the price sits from fundamental value (p. 3).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"171\"><strong>Funding liquidity</strong> = how easily traders can get funding. In the model, it is the speculators’ scarcity or <strong>shadow cost of capital</strong> (p. 3).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"172\"><strong>Margin or haircut</strong> = price minus collateral value; \"must be financed with the trader’ s own capital\" (p. 1). \"The total margin on all positions cannot exceed a trader’ s capital at any time\" (p. 1).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"173\" id=\"b173\">4.2 The model in plain terms (pp. 7–10)</h2>\n<table data-b=\"174\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.10.</strong> Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009): 4.2 The model in plain terms (pp. 7–10)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Element</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Setup</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Time</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">t = 0, 1, 2, 3; securities pay off at t = 3; fundamental volatility follows an ARCH process (bad shocks raise future volatility)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Customers</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Three risk-averse agents with offsetting endowment shocks, arriving <em>one at a time</em>. That creates a temporary order imbalance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Speculators</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk-neutral dealers or hedge funds that absorb the imbalance (provide immediacy), subject to a margin constraint: total margin ≤ capital (eq. 4, p. 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Financiers</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Set margins to cover a value-at-risk at probability π (about 1%) (eqs. 6–7, p. 8–9). They are either <strong>informed</strong> (know fundamentals) or <strong>uninformed</strong> (see only prices)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Illiquidity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The gap between price and fundamental value, Δ (eq. 8, p. 10)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"175\" id=\"b175\">4.3 The key results (pp. 10–20)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"176\"><strong>Proposition 1 (p. 10).</strong> Market illiquidity equals the asset’ s margin times the speculators’ shadow cost of funding minus one: Δ = m × (φ − 1). So \"a security’ s market illiquidity is the product of its margin and the shadow cost of funding\" (p. 4).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"177\"><strong>Proposition 2: stabilizing margins (p. 11).</strong> If financiers know that prices are temporarily off and will return to fundamentals, margins fall when prices are further from fundamentals (the \"cushioning effect\"). This is a benchmark, not the real-world case.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"178\"><strong>Proposition 3: destabilizing margins (p. 12).</strong> If financiers are uninformed and volatility clusters, they read price swings as fundamental risk and <em>raise</em> margins when illiquidity rises. This matches real margin-setting, which is \"primarily based on volatility estimates from past price movements\", and it \"introduces a procyclicality that amplifies funding shocks—a major criticism of the Basel II capital regulation\" (p. 12). <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: procyclicality and Basel II<span>]</span></strong></p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"179\"><strong>Proposition 4: fragility (p. 13).</strong> Liquidity is \"fragile\" if the equilibrium price cannot be continuous in shocks: a tiny change in funding or fundamentals causes a jump in illiquidity. This arises when destabilizing margins or large existing positions (loss effect) make the speculators’ demand curve slope upward in price (pp. 13–14).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"180\">Worked example (pp. 13–16): volatility parameters σ = 10, θ = 0.3, wealth W = 900, VaR π = 1%. Panel A of Figure 3 (p. 14) shows two stable equilibria, a liquid one at price 120 and an illiquid one near 94. With a wealth shock of −150 (panel B) only the illiquid equilibrium survives (p. 15).</p></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"181\"><strong>Proposition 5: liquidity spirals (p. 17).</strong> If the capital constraint is slack, price equals fundamentals and is insensitive to wealth. In an illiquid equilibrium, the price sensitivity to speculator wealth is amplified by two loops:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"182\"><strong>Margin (haircut) spiral:</strong> funding shock → less liquidity provision → bigger price swing → higher margins → tighter funding → repeat (pp. 3–4, 18).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"183\"><strong>Loss spiral:</strong> funding shock → illiquidity → losses on existing positions → forced selling → further price drop (pp. 3–4).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"184\">The two together are \"greater than the sum of their separate effects\" (p. 18). The algebra: each term of an infinite series is one loop around the circle in Figure 2 (p. 18).</p></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"185\"><strong>Proposition 6: cross-section (pp. 19–20).</strong> (i) <em>Commonality</em>: illiquidities of different securities co-move with each other and with the funding shadow cost. (ii) <em>Commonality of fragility</em>: jumps happen at the same time across assets that speculators trade. (iii) <em>Quality and liquidity</em>: lower-volatility assets are more liquid. (iv) <em>Flight to quality</em>: when funding tightens, the liquidity gap between high-margin and low-margin assets widens (Figure 5, p. 21).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"186\">\"Paradoxically, liquidity spirals imply that a larger shock to the customers’ demand for immediacy leads to a reduction in the provision of immediacy during such times of stress.\" (p. 4) <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: why liquidity vanishes when needed<span>]</span></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"187\" id=\"b187\">4.4 Liquidity risk before the crisis (pp. 22–25)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"188\">Even when the constraint is not binding at t = 0, the <em>risk</em> that it may bind at t = 1 reduces liquidity provision and lowers prices (p. 22–24).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"189\"><strong>Pricing kernel (eq. 31, p. 23):</strong> p0 = E0<span>[</span>p1<span>]</span> + Cov(φ1, p1)/E0<span>[</span>φ1<span>]</span>. Assets that pay little when funding is scarce (φ high) must sell at a discount.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"190\"><strong>Skewness (pp. 24–25):</strong> the funding constraint makes speculator returns negatively skewed and prices fat-tailed, because losses are amplified by spirals but gains are not.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"191\" id=\"b191\">4.5 Historical illustrations the paper uses</h2>\n<table data-b=\"192\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.11.</strong> Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009): 4.5 Historical illustrations the paper uses</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Episode</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Point</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Margins on S&amp;P 500 futures (1987, 1990, 1998, 2007)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Margins rose in each liquidity crisis (Figure 1)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1987 crash</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Thirteen NYSE specialist units \"had no buying power\" (SEC 1988); banks raised haircuts, e.g. 20% to 25%, and made intraday margin calls</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">18–19</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Russia default / LTCM 1998</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A \"trivial wealth shock relative to global arbitrage capital\" had a large effect; LTCM was taken over by fourteen banks</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">17, 23</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2007–08</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Subprime losses of several hundred billion dollars (about 5% of stock market value) became more than $8 trillion of market loss once borne by levered institutions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bear Stearns (March 2008)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Clients terminated brokerage relationships, a run on the investment bank</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">30</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"193\" id=\"b193\">4.6 Links to banks and Basel (Appendix A.2, pp. 30–31)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"194\">Banks face a funding constraint \"in the spirit of Equation (4)\" in stress (p. 30).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"195\"><strong>Basel I mapped onto the model.</strong> Capital must exceed 8% of risk-weighted assets, with risk weights 0% (cash, government securities), 50% (mortgage-backed loans) and 100% (other). \"The requirement… corresponds to Equation (4) with margins of 0%, 4%, and 8%, respectively\" (p. 30). <strong><span>[</span>QUOTE: capital regulation as a margin<span>]</span></strong></p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"196\">The 1996 amendment let banks use internal VaR models; banks then built off-balance-sheet vehicles (SIVs, conduits) (pp. 30–31).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"197\">Margins and haircuts are \"used interchangeably\" (p. 29).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"198\">SEC net capital rule (a 6⅔% of aggregate indebtedness limit, about 15:1 leverage) works similarly (p. 31).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"199\" id=\"b199\">4.7 Testable predictions and policy (pp. 25–27)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"200\">Speculator capital and volatility (VIX) are state variables for liquidity and risk premia; the effect is nonlinear (strong when capital is low).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"201\">Margins should co-move with illiquidity, and commonality should be higher when funding is tight.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"202\">Policy (p. 27): \"Central banks can help mitigate market liquidity problems by controlling funding liquidity\", for example by signalling extra funding in a crisis, which loosens margins (the NY Fed did so in 1987).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"203\" id=\"b203\">4.8 Limits when you cite it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"204\">It models <strong>traders and market makers</strong>, not retail banks with deposits. The bank link is made only in Appendix A.2 (p. 30).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"205\"><strong>No network of counterparties.</strong> Contagion works through <em>prices and margins</em>, not through bilateral debts. That is the complement to Eisenberg–Noe and Acemoglu et al.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"206\">One security in the main example; the two-security case is an extension (pp. 21–22).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"207\">Parameters and figures are illustrative, not calibrated. Several claims (fat tails, positive margins at t = 0) are shown numerically with simulations \"available upon request\" (p. 25, fn 12).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"208\">Final-journal pagination differs (see the file note).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"209\" id=\"b209\">4.9 Quotables from this paper</h2>\n<table data-b=\"210\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.12.</strong> Brunnermeier and Pedersen (2009): 4.9 Quotables from this paper</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote or point</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Use in thesis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Abstract: market and funding liquidity are mutually reinforcing; liquidity spirals</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Definition and framing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Total margin cannot exceed capital</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Funding-constraint logic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Illiquidity = margin × (shadow cost of funding − 1), Prop. 1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4, 10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Formal link</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Margin spiral and loss spiral (Figure 2)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3–4, 18</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fire-sale and haircut channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Margins based on past volatility \"introduces a procyclicality… a major criticism of the Basel II capital regulation\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Procyclicality of capital rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fragility: a small shock causes a jump in illiquidity (Prop. 4)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">13</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Non-linear stress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Paradoxically… a larger shock… leads to a reduction in the provision of immediacy\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why liquidity vanishes in stress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel 8% capital requirement is equivalent to margins of 0%, 4%, 8%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bridge to Layer 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Central banks can mitigate by controlling funding liquidity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">27</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Policy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"211\"><strong>Why this matters for the thesis.</strong> If transition risk lowers collateral values of carbon-intensive assets, lenders may raise haircuts on them, which tightens funding and forces sales (a margin spiral). This is my inference from the paper’ s mechanism, not a claim the authors make.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"212\" id=\"b212\">5. Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis</h1>\n<p data-b=\"213\">Page numbers: Allen–Gale = JPE pp. 1–33; Eisenberg–Noe = <em>Management Science</em> pp. 236–249 (OCR-read); Acemoglu et al. = AER pp. 564–608; Brunnermeier–Pedersen = Advance Access PDF pp. 1–38 (see the file note in section 4).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"214\" id=\"b214\">5.1 Ten quotes or points to reach for first</h2>\n<table data-b=\"215\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.13.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis: 5.1 Ten quotes or points to reach for first</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Point</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Paper and page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it helps</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A small shock in one region can spread through the whole economy; contagion depends on how complete the claims structure is</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale, p. 1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining contagion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cross-holdings cannot raise total liquidity; mutual liquidation \"merely denies liquidity\" to the troubled region</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale, pp. 4–5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why interbank links amplify</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Contagion depends on the endogenous pattern of financial claims\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale, p. 24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Structure, not size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A central bank can be seen as completing markets</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale, p. 32</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lender-of-last-resort argument</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Each node pays \"the minimum of what it has and what it owes\"; clearing vector exists and is unique under regularity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eisenberg–Noe, pp. 240–242</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Method for any network stress test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The round in which a bank defaults measures its systemic exposure (fictitious default algorithm, at most n rounds)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eisenberg–Noe, pp. 243–244</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Default-wave stress testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dense networks stabilise small shocks but propagate large ones: the phase transition</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acemoglu et al., pp. 564, 578</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Framing for climate shocks of uncertain size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Exposure limits designed for small shocks \"may be counterproductive if the shocks are in fact large\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acemoglu et al., p. 587</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Policy design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Illiquidity = margin × (shadow cost of funding − 1); margin and loss spirals</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Brunnermeier–Pedersen, pp. 3–4, 10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fire-sale and haircut channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Margins based on past volatility create procyclicality, \"a major criticism of the Basel II capital regulation\"; the Basel 8% rule maps to margins of 0%, 4% and 8%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Brunnermeier–Pedersen, pp. 12, 30</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital rules and procyclicality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"216\" id=\"b216\">5.2 Four papers, four contagion channels</h2>\n<table data-b=\"217\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.14.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis: 5.2 Four papers, four contagion channels</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Paper</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Channel</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Link type</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Shock that matters</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Main takeaway</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale (2000)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Liquidity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Interbank deposits</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Aggregate liquidity shortage</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Chain structure spreads contagion; complete and disconnected contain it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eisenberg–Noe (2001)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Solvency (payments)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Interbank debts, any network</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Any fall in outside cash flows</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Clearing vector exists and is found in at most n rounds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acemoglu et al. (2015)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Solvency and liquidation</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unsecured interbank loans</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Size and number of shocks</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Phase transition: dense is safe only for small shocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Brunnermeier–Pedersen (2009)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Market and funding liquidity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Prices, margins, haircuts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Funding or capital shock to liquidity providers</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Spirals; fragility; commonality across assets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"218\">Acemoglu et al. say themselves that fire sales and withdrawal of liquidity are \"open question<span>[</span>s<span>]</span> for future research\" (p. 587). Brunnermeier–Pedersen is the paper that studies them. Together the four cover both direct (counterparty) and indirect (price and funding) channels.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"219\" id=\"b219\">5.3 How Layer 4 connects to the other layers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"220\"><strong>Layer 3 (capital).</strong> The capital stack is the <em>buffer</em> in these models: Allen–Gale’ s buffer b(ω) (p. 19), Acemoglu et al.’ s \"excess liquidity\" a − v (p. 574) and Eisenberg–Noe’ s equity (p. 239). Brunnermeier–Pedersen show that a capital rule such as Basel’ s 8% acts like a margin (p. 30). Borio et al. (Layer 3) made the \"tsunami, not dominoes\" point; Layer 4 supplies the formal domino model.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"221\"><strong>Layer 6 (climate).</strong> Climate transition shocks hit many banks together (large p) and can be large (large ε), which in Acemoglu et al. is the regime where dense linkages amplify losses (pp. 578, 582–583). Brunnermeier–Pedersen’ s margin spiral gives a channel through which higher haircuts on high-carbon collateral could force sales. Both are inferences for your thesis; none of these four papers mentions climate.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"222\"><strong>Layer 5 (empirics).</strong> Eisenberg–Noe warn that changes in total asset values can mislead for interconnected firms (pp. 237–238), which matters if you run event studies on bank returns.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"223\" id=\"b223\">5.4 What I read, and what I did not</h2>\n<table data-b=\"224\">\n<caption><strong>Table 5.15.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 4 feeds the thesis: 5.4 What I read, and what I did not</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Paper</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Read</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Not read in detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Allen–Gale</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">All 33 pages</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nothing skipped; references only skimmed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eisenberg–Noe</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">All 14 pages, via OCR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Appendix 1 proof (p. 248–249) checked for structure only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acemoglu et al.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pages 564–588 (all main text)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Appendix A (harmonic distance) read only to its first result, Appendix B proofs (to p. 608) and the online appendix not read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Brunnermeier–Pedersen</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pages 1–33 (text, appendices A and B)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Rest of the proofs of Proposition 6 and references</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"225\">Flags: (1) Eisenberg–Noe has a damaged text layer, so quotes came from OCR; (2) Brunnermeier–Pedersen is the Advance Access PDF, so journal page numbers may differ; (3) the few formulae in my notes (for example harmonic distance and the shock thresholds) were written from the text, so check the symbols against the paper before you write equations into the thesis.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"226\" id=\"b226\">5.5 Suggested use in the thesis</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"227\">In the literature review, present the four papers as <strong>channels</strong> (liquidity, payments, network structure, funding), not as four separate stories.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"228\">In the theory chapter, use the Acemoglu et al. phase-transition result to argue that the effect of interconnection on climate stress is conditional on the shock size.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"229\">In the Indian context, state clearly that none of these papers tests Indian banks. The link is conceptual, and any claim about public sector banks needs evidence from your own data (Layer 3 sources, RBI reports).</p></li>\n</ol>\n","s":[[102,"b1"],[103,"b18"],[103,"b21"],[104,"b27"],[104,"b30"],[105,"b36"],[105,"b48"],[106,"b54"],[106,"b57"],[106,"b62"],[107,"b67"],[107,"b70"],[107,"b74"],[108,"b78"],[108,"b86"],[109,"b95"],[109,"b102"],[109,"b106"],[110,"b111"],[110,"b113"],[110,"b116"],[111,"b120"],[111,"b126"],[112,"b136"],[113,"b149"],[113,"b154"],[113,"b159"],[114,"b162"],[114,"b165"],[115,"b169"],[115,"b173"],[115,"b175"],[116,"b187"],[116,"b191"],[117,"b193"],[117,"b199"],[117,"b203"],[118,"b209"],[118,"b212"],[118,"b214"],[119,"b216"],[120,"b219"],[120,"b223"],[121,"b226"]]};